        "content": "<p>netliomax25-code opened <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/14747\">PR #14747</a> from <code>netliomax25-code:wasi-http-authority-userinfo</code> to <code>bytecodealliance:main</code>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li><code>request.set-authority</code> accepted an RFC 3986 userinfo subcomponent such as <code>user@example.com</code>: the <code>http</code> crate's <code>Authority</code> parses it, and the shared <code>parse_authority</code> helper only validated the port.</li>\n<li>Both the p2 and p3 outgoing request paths emit the authority verbatim as the <code>Host</code> header, which guests cannot set directly because <code>host</code> is a forbidden header, and RFC 9110 defines <code>Host</code> as <code>uri-host [\":\" port]</code> and forbids a sender from generating userinfo within a message.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>An <code>@</code> can only occur in an authority as the userinfo delimiter, and percent-encoded forms are already rejected by the <code>http</code> crate, so <code>parse_authority</code> now rejects any authority containing one. Unit tests cover the rejected forms, and the p2/p3 guest test programs assert <code>set-authority</code> fails for them.<br>\n</p>\n</blockquote>",
